On Thu, 17 Aug 2006, rpresser wrote:
>
> It sure is annoying when you're hitting ESC to skip thousands of pline
> messages and you end up dead with no way to see why.  So I wrote this
> patch. Everything that would appear on the top line gets written to a
> text file, "messagelog.txt", in the current directory. Menus, long
> listings, help, etc. is not written.
>
> I've only tested with win32 tty, but it's such a simple patch I don't
> see why it wouldn't work in any environment that you can write to a
> file.
>
> Comments solicited.
>

> +void
> +messagelog(line)
> +const char *line;

   You might check whether 'const' should be a #define in this case;
I think Nethack may be portable to systems that pre-date 'const'.
(I can't check the source myself right now, or I would.)

> +{
> +    FILE *fp;
> +    fp = fopen("messagelog.txt","a");

   Should be a static variable, as someone already said. Also, you need
to check for success --- what if the user doesn't have write permission
to that file? Unixy systems probably have ways to check permissions
ahead of time, lock files against deletion while they're open, and so
on, which you'd need in order to make this safe and portable. (Doesn't
matter for a homebrew patch, but if you wanted the DevTeam to look at
it, it would probably matter.)

     static FILE *fp = NULL;
     static int first = 1;
     if (first) {
         first = 0;
         fp = fopen("messagelog.txt", "w");
         if (!fp) pline("You feel worried about your message log.");
     }
     if (fp) {
         fputs(line, fp);
         putc('\n', fp);
     }

...and then some function invocation at endgame to fclose the file.


> C:\nethack> type messagelog.txt
> Velkommen Rachel, welcome to NetHack!  You are a neutral human
> Valkyrie.
> You see here a spear.

   Yikes... there had better be an in-game way to erase the message log,
or you'll fill up your disk real fast!

   Also, it would be better if this were a configurable (or even 
toggleable?) option. (What if I just want to record my rampage through
the Planes, and nothing before then?)

-Arthur


